<statement>
<p>Define the autocovariance function.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Define the autocorrelation function.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Define white noise.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Define an AR(1) process.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Define an MA(1) process.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Define an ARMA(<m>p,q</m>) process.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain the meaning of ARIMA(<m>p,d,q</m>).</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Define first differencing.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Define seasonal differencing.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Define the PACF conceptually.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Define a forecast error.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>For</p>

<p>
<md>X_t
=
0.4X_{t-1}
+
\varepsilon_t,</md>
</p>

<p>determine whether the AR(1) process is stationary.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>For</p>

<p>
<md>X_t
=
1.2X_{t-1}
+
\varepsilon_t,</md>
</p>

<p>determine whether the AR(1) process is stationary.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>X_t
=
2
+
0.5X_{t-1}
+
\varepsilon_t.</md>
</p>

<p>Find the stationary mean.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Suppose an AR(1) has</p>

<p>
<md>\phi=0.8</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\sigma_\varepsilon^2=9.</md>
</p>

<p>Find the stationary variance.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>For the previous AR(1), compute</p>

<p>
<md>\rho(1),
\quad
\rho(2),
\quad
\rho(3).</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>X_t
=
\varepsilon_t
+
0.5\varepsilon_{t-1}.</md>
</p>

<p>Find the variance.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>For the previous MA(1), compute <m>\rho(1)</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>For the previous MA(1), find <m>\rho(2)</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Classify a random walk as an ARIMA model.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Write</p>

<p>
<md>X_t-X_{t-12}</md>
</p>

<p>using the backshift operator.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Expand</p>

<p>
<md>(1-B)^2X_t.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>For a monthly series with annual seasonality, state the seasonal period.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>If the AR(1) stationary mean is <m>\mu=20</m>, <m>\phi=0.7</m>, and
<m>X_t=25</m>, find the one-step forecast.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>For the previous model, find the two-step forecast.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>For a random walk with innovation variance <m>4</m>, find the variance of a
five-step forecast error.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Suppose simple exponential smoothing uses</p>

<p>
<md>\alpha=0.3,</md>
</p>

<p>with</p>

<p>
<md>X_t=20,
\qquad
\ell_{t-1}=18.</md>
</p>

<p>Find <m>\ell_t</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Derive the stationary mean of an AR(1) process.</p>
